  "answer": "No. The tuned controller (feasible_best) was not safer: the default linear MPC failed less, and the pooled 90% ranges of the two controllers separate. feasible_best failed in every pair, on all four scenes, and linear failed in none. The sign test over pairs where only one controller failed points the same way. Two things limit this. First, none of feasible_best's failures involved the pedestrian. On a RIGHT command it turned too sharply into parked cars or steered left off the road. It did this even at the recorded timing and speed, so the result shows that feasible_best is broken in these scenes, not that it handles early pedestrians worse. Second, the study stopped after two rounds and only tried step-outs of 0 to 0.5 s early at 1.0x to 1.5x speed. Most settings have one pair each, so how linear handles a pedestrian 1 to 2 s early is still untested.",
